  "worked_solution": "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =({2}^{(-x)}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{(-x)}}) \\\\  & =({(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-\\frac{1}{(1/2)^{x}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}}-{2}^{x}) \\\\  & =-({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}}) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$",
